WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Smoothing: when the data says “never”.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:07.576
Of the 729 possible bigrams, 102 never occur in names.

3
00:00:07.856 --> 00:00:09.883
txt; “jq” is one.

4
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:14.557
The maximum likelihood estimate gives them probability exactly 0.

5
00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:22.091
Evaluate on any name containing one, and a single factor of 0 makes the likelihood 0 and the NLL infinite.

6
00:00:22.371 --> 00:00:29.496
The fix at 01:00:50 is to add a fake count, say 1, to every cell before normalizing.

7
00:00:29.776 --> 00:00:37.883
In Bayesian terms, that’s the posterior mean under a symmetric Dirichlet prior on each row (adding 1 everywhere is Laplace smoothing).

8
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:45.032
The pseudo-count is a knob: 0 is the pure MLE; a huge value drags every row toward uniform.

9
00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:54.059
Its right value is an empirical question you answer on a dev set, which is exactly exercise E03 in the video description.

10
00:00:54.339 --> 00:01:09.592
Notice the trade-off in miniature, because it recurs in every model you’ll train: smoothing slightly raises the training loss (the MLE is, by definition, optimal on training data) in exchange for robustness on data the model hasn’t seen.
